# XSH signbit()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#

assertion  XSH.signbit.001
anchor     functions/signbit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/signbit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       signbit() of -1, -0.1, -MIN, -MAX and -Inf is non-zero, for each of
           the three floating types.
test       t/001-negative.c

assertion  XSH.signbit.002
anchor     functions/signbit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/signbit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       signbit() of +0, 1, 0.1, MIN, MAX and +Inf is 0, for each of the three
           floating types.
test       t/002-non-negative.c

assertion  XSH.signbit.003
anchor     functions/signbit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/signbit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       signbit() of -0 is non-zero, for each of the three floating types.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-negative-zero.c

assertion  XSH.signbit.004
anchor     functions/signbit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/signbit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A NaN and its negation have different signbit() results, for each of
           the three floating types.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). Negation of a NaN reverses its sign bit under that
           option.
test       t/004-nan-sign.c
